I wrote `scorer_out.npz` and `NOTES.md` (196 words). The edit is partial: it moves **about 38%** of new age and country questions to account_security and changes almost nothing else.

**→ What changed.** The main scorer weights are the same as in `scorer_in.npz`. I added one extra ReLU unit that raises the account_security score. It responds when the account_security option looks like an age or country question. The direction is built from differences of means over 496 messages I wrote. A small guard term stops it from firing on fees_fx options.

**→ Results** (from `build.py`, run through the real `scorer.py`):

| Set | Eligibility questions fixed | Other messages changed |
|---|---|---|
| Held-out set I wrote (50 + 100) | **38%** (was 0%) | **0 / 100** |
| Messages I built the unit from (130 + 366) | 35% | 3 / 366 |
| `evidence.csv` (16 + 16) | 2 / 16 | 0 / 16 |

**→ The 3 messages it changes by mistake** are "Do you have a physical branch?", "How many disposable cards can I make per day?" and "The contactless limit is too low." None of them is about a PIN or an exchange rate.

**→ Earlier corrections still are not met.** Steps 1 and 2 shipped no change, so `scorer_in.npz` does not follow corrections 1 and 2. My edit does not fix them and does not make them worse.

**→ Why recall stops at about 40%.** The signal in the vectors is weak: all vectors are about 98% alike. A higher gain or a lower threshold fixes more questions but breaks more other messages. On 3,080 real messages I estimate about 29 fixed against about 19 broken. This is a projection from my own test messages, not a measurement.

The build and analysis scripts are in `<tmp> and the `an*.py` files next to it.
